      "ICE table: initial [N₂O₄] = 0.20, [NO₂] = 0. Change: −x and +2x. Equilibrium: 0.20 − x and 2x.",
      "Set up Kc: (2x)² / (0.20 − x) = 5.9×10⁻³.",
      "Since Kc is small, approximate 0.20 − x ≈ 0.20: 4x² / 0.20 = 5.9×10⁻³ → x² = 2.95×10⁻⁴.",
      "x = 0.017 M. Check the approximation: 0.017/0.20 = 8.6%… above 5%, so it is better to solve the quadratic.",
      "Quadratic: 4x² + 5.9×10⁻³x − 1.18×10⁻³ = 0 → x = 0.0164 M.",
      "[NO₂] = 2x = 0.033 M and [N₂O₄] = 0.184 M.",
    ],
    exampleAnswer: "[NO₂] ≈ 0.033 M at equilibrium (the approximation would give 0.034 M, very close).",
    errors: [
      "Including pure solids or liquids in the Kc expression.",
      "Forgetting to raise the concentrations to the stoichiometric coefficients.",
      "Using initial concentrations in Kc instead of the equilibrium ones.",
      "Accepting a negative root or one greater than the initial concentration when solving the quadratic.",
    ],
    tip: "If K is very small (< 10⁻³ approx.), the change x is usually negligible compared with the initial concentration: approximate, calculate and CHECK that x < 5% of the initial value.",
  ),
  "termo-cinetica": TranslatedTopic(
    title: "Thermochemistry and kinetics",
    level: "General Chemistry II",
    summary: [
      "The enthalpy of reaction (ΔH) can be calculated with Hess's law (sum of steps), with enthalpies of formation: ΔH° = Σ n·ΔHf°(products) − Σ n·ΔHf°(reactants), or by breaking and forming bonds. A negative sign = exothermic.",
      "Entropy (ΔS) grows with disorder: more moles of gas, larger volume, dissolution of solids. Spontaneity is decided by ΔG = ΔH − TΔS: ΔG < 0, spontaneous.",
      "In kinetics, the rate law v = k[A]ᵐ[B]ⁿ is determined experimentally; the orders do not come from the balanced equation. The activation energy (Arrhenius) explains why temperature speeds reactions up so much.",
    ],
    formulas: [
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Hess / formation", expr: "ΔH° = Σ prod. − Σ react.", note: "Always multiplying by the coefficients"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Gibbs", expr: "ΔG = ΔH − T·ΔS", note: "ΔG < 0 → spontaneous; T in kelvin"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Rate law", expr: "v = k [A]ᵐ [B]ⁿ", note: "m and n are determined experimentally"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Arrhenius", expr: "k = A · e^(−Ea/RT)", note: "When T rises, k grows exponentially"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "First order", expr: "ln[A] = ln[A]₀ − k·t", note: "And t½ = ln 2 / k, independent of [A]₀"),
    ],
    exampleTitle: "ΔH° of combustion of CH₄ with ΔHf°: CO₂ = −393.5, H₂O(l) = −285.8, CH₄ = −74.8 kJ/mol.",
    exampleSteps: [
      "Reaction: CH₄ + 2 O₂ → CO₂ + 2 H₂O. ΔHf°(O₂) = 0 (element in its standard form).",
      "Products: (−393.5) + 2(−285.8) = −965.1 kJ.",
      "Reactants: (−74.8) + 2(0) = −74.8 kJ.",
      "ΔH° = −965.1 − (−74.8) = −890.3 kJ per mole of CH₄.",
    ],
    exampleAnswer: "ΔH° ≈ −890 kJ/mol: a strongly exothermic combustion.",
    errors: [
      "Forgetting to multiply ΔHf° by the stoichiometric coefficients.",
      "Assigning ΔHf° ≠ 0 to an element in its standard state (O₂, N₂, graphite carbon are 0).",
      "Deducing reaction orders from the coefficients of the overall equation.",
      "Using °C in ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.",
    ],
    tip: "In thermochemistry, the sign IS half the answer: before handing it in, say out loud “exothermic/endothermic, spontaneous/not” and check that it matches your sign.",
  ),
  "organica": TranslatedTopic(
