  "evidencias-reaccion": TranslatedTopic(
    title: "Evidence of a chemical reaction",
    level: "General Chemistry I",
    summary: [
      "A chemical reaction is a reorganization of atoms: bonds break and new ones form, with new substances at the end. Since atoms cannot be seen, we rely on observable evidence.",
      "The five classic pieces of evidence are: evolution of a gas (bubbles, effervescence), formation of a precipitate (a solid appearing in a liquid), color change, temperature change (the mixture heats up or cools down) and emission of light.",
      "Careful: a physical change can look like evidence. Ice melting changes state but is still water; sugar dissolving does not change the substance. Evidence suggests a reaction, but the confirmation is that substances with new properties appear.",
    ],
    formulas: [
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Gas", expr: "Effervescence / bubbles", note: "E.g.: CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + CO₂↑ + H₂O"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Precipitate", expr: "Solid that appears", note: "E.g.: AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl↓ + NaNO₃"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Color change", expr: "New permanent color", note: "E.g.: Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu (blue → green)"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Temperature", expr: "Heats up or cools down", note: "E.g.: H₂SO₄ + water heats up a lot"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Light", expr: "Emission of light", note: "E.g.: combustion, chemiluminescence"),
    ],
    exampleTitle: "Is there a reaction when vinegar (acetic acid) is mixed with sodium bicarbonate?",
    exampleSteps: [
      "Observe: intense effervescence appears (bubbles) and the mixture cools slightly.",
      "The effervescence is gas: CO₂ being released.",
      "The equation: CH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CH₃COONa + CO₂↑ + H₂O.",
      "New substances form (sodium acetate, CO₂, water): it is a chemical reaction.",
    ],
    exampleAnswer: "Yes: CO₂ is released, clear evidence of a chemical reaction.",
    errors: [
      "Calling a change of state a reaction: melting ice is not a reaction.",
      "Saying “smoke comes out” when it is water vapor: smoke is from combustion.",
      "Forgetting that the color change from dissolving a dye is not a reaction: the dye is still the same.",
    ],
    tip: "In the exam, name the evidence AND the equation: “CO₂ is released (gas), therefore…” is worth more than just “it bubbles”.",
  ),
  "identificacion-iones": TranslatedTopic(
    title: "Identifying ions",
    level: "Analytical Chemistry",
    summary: [
      "Identifying an ion means recognizing it by a characteristic reaction: a reagent that only reacts with that ion (or almost only) and gives an unmistakable product: a precipitate of a certain color, a gas with a certain smell, or a flame color.",
      "Cations are identified mainly by precipitation and by flame color: Ag⁺ gives a white precipitate with Cl⁻, Ba²⁺ gives a white precipitate with SO₄²⁻, Fe³⁺ gives a blood-red color with SCN⁻, and Cu²⁺ gives an intense blue with ammonia.",
      "Anions are identified with precipitation reactions and gas evolution: Cl⁻ with AgNO₃ (white precipitate), SO₄²⁻ with BaCl₂ (white precipitate), CO₃²⁻ with an acid (CO₂ bubbles) and NO₃⁻ with the brown ring test.",
      "Classic qualitative analysis is a systematic scheme: cations are separated into groups with group reagents (HCl, H₂S, (NH₄)₂CO₃…) and confirmed one by one. In the exam, the usual questions are about the most typical identification reactions.",
    ],
    formulas: [
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Ag⁺", expr: "Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ (white)", note: "Redissolves in NH₃: confirms Cl⁻"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Ba²⁺", expr: "Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓ (white)", note: "Precipitate insoluble in acids"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Fe³⁺", expr: "Fe³⁺ + SCN⁻ → [Fe(SCN)]²⁺ (blood red)", note: "Very intense color, very sensitive"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Cu²⁺", expr: "Cu²⁺ + NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ (intense blue)", note: "The blue precipitate redissolves in excess NH₃"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "NH₄⁺", expr: "NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ (pungent smell)", note: "Heat it: NH₃ smells and turns damp paper blue"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Pb²⁺", expr: "Pb²⁺ + 2I⁻ → PbI₂↓ (yellow)", note: "Characteristic yellow precipitate"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Cl⁻", expr: "Cl⁻ + Ag⁺ → AgCl↓ (white)", note: "Darkens in light"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "SO₄²⁻", expr: "SO₄²⁻ + Ba²⁺ → BaSO₄↓ (white)", note: "Insoluble in HCl: distinguishes it from CO₃²⁻"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "CO₃²⁻", expr: "CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂↑ + H₂O", note: "Effervescence; CO₂ turns limewater cloudy"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "NO₃⁻", expr: "Brown ring with FeSO₄ + H₂SO₄", note: "Brown ring test"),
    ],
    exampleTitle: "A solution gives a white precipitate with AgNO₃ that redissolves in ammonia. Which anion is present?",
    exampleSteps: [
      "The white precipitate with Ag⁺ indicates a halide (Cl⁻, Br⁻ or I⁻) or CO₃²⁻.",
      "AgCl is white and redissolves in NH₃, forming [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺.",
      "AgBr is cream and AgI is yellow: they are not pure white.",
      "Silver carbonate is also white, but it dissolves in acid with effervescence, not in ammonia.",
    ],
    exampleAnswer: "The anion is Cl⁻: the precipitate is AgCl, white and soluble in ammonia.",
    errors: [
      "Saying just “white precipitate”: BaSO₄ and AgCl are both white, but they are distinguished with NH₃ or with HCl.",
      "Forgetting the confirmation: an identification needs two tests, not one.",
      "Confusing the flame test (identifies the cation) with precipitation (identifies the anion).",
    ],
    tip: "In identification problems, make a table: suspected ion → reagent → observable product. The one that fits all the tests is the ion.",
  ),
  "reacciones-inorganicas": TranslatedTopic(
    title: "Inorganic chemistry reactions",
    level: "Inorganic Chemistry",
    summary: [
      "Inorganic chemistry is organized into a few families of reactions. Learning them is learning the language of chemistry: formation of oxides, formation of salts, thermal decomposition and reactions with O₂, H₂ and CO.",
      "Oxides form when an element reacts with oxygen: metals give basic oxides (Na₂O with water gives NaOH) and nonmetals give acidic oxides (SO₃ with water gives H₂SO₄).",
      "Salts form by neutralization (acid + base), by reaction of a metal with an acid (metal + acid → salt + H₂) or of a basic oxide with an acid. Thermal decomposition breaks a compound apart with heat: carbonates give oxide + CO₂ and chlorates give chloride + O₂.",
      "O₂ oxidizes almost everything (combustion and oxidation of metals), H₂ is a reducing agent that strips oxygen from many oxides (reduction), and CO is the industrial reducing agent par excellence: in the blast furnace it reduces Fe₂O₃ to iron.",
    ],
    formulas: [
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Metal oxide", expr: "2 Mg + O₂ → 2 MgO", note: "Basic oxide: MgO + H₂O → Mg(OH)₂"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Nonmetal oxide", expr: "S + O₂ → SO₂", note: "Acidic oxide: SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Salt by neutralization", expr: "HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O", note: "Acid + base → salt + water"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Salt by metal + acid", expr: "Zn + 2 HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂↑", note: "Only metals above H in the activity series"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Carbonate decomposition", expr: "CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂↑", note: "With strong heat (calcination)"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Chlorate decomposition", expr: "2 KClO₃ → 2 KCl + 3 O₂↑", note: "With heat and MnO₂ as catalyst"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "With O₂ (combustion)", expr: "CH₄ + 2 O₂ → CO₂ + 2 H₂O", note: "Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "With H₂ (reduction)", expr: "CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O", note: "H₂ reduces the oxide to metal"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "With CO (blast furnace)", expr: "Fe₂O₃ + 3 CO → 2 Fe + 3 CO₂", note: "CO reduces the iron ore"),
