  ),
  "periodicidad": TranslatedTopic(
    title: "Periodicity",
    level: "General Chemistry II",
    summary: [
      "The properties of the elements repeat periodically because the electron configuration repeats: each period adds a shell and each group shares the valence electrons.",
      "The atomic radius decreases from left to right across a period (the effective nuclear charge grows and pulls the cloud in more) and increases down a group (a new electron shell is added). The effective nuclear charge, Zeff, is the charge a valence electron actually feels: Z minus the shielding of the inner electrons.",
      "The ionization energy (energy to remove an electron) increases from left to right and decreases down a group: the closer to the nucleus and the less shielded the electron is, the harder it is to remove. Electron affinity (energy released when gaining an electron) and electronegativity (attraction of the bonding electrons) follow the same trend: they increase upwards and to the right, with fluorine as the maximum.",
      "Metallic character increases downwards and to the left: metals lose electrons easily (low ionization energy), nonmetals gain them. That is why the elements on the B–Si–As–Te–At diagonal are metalloids.",
    ],
    formulas: [
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Atomic radius", expr: "Decreases across a period · increases down a group", note: "Zeff grows across the period and squeezes the cloud"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Ionization energy", expr: "Increases across a period · decreases down a group", note: "Maxima: noble gases; minima: alkali metals"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Electron affinity", expr: "Increases upwards and to the right", note: "Maximum: Cl (F is smaller and repels more)"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Electronegativity", expr: "Increases across a period · decreases down a group", note: "Maximum: F (3.98); minimum: Cs and Fr (~0.7)"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Metallic character", expr: "Increases downwards and to the left", note: "Metals lose electrons; nonmetals gain them"),
      TranslatedFormula(name: "Effective nuclear charge", expr: "Zeff = Z − S", note: "S = shielding by the inner electrons"),
    ],
    exampleTitle: "Order from lowest to highest ionization energy: Na, Mg, Al, Si.",
    exampleSteps: [
      "They are in the same period (n = 3), so the effective nuclear charge rules.",
      "In general, ionization energy grows with Z across the period: Na < Mg < Al < Si.",
      "But there is an anomaly: Mg (1s²2s²2p⁶3s²) has the 3s subshell full and is more stable than expected, and Al (…3p¹) has a 3p electron that is easy to remove.",
      "That is why the real first ionization energies are: Al (578) < Mg (738) < Si (787)… and Na is the lowest (496).",
      "Real order: Na (496) < Al (578) < Mg (738) < Si (787) kJ/mol.",
    ],
    exampleAnswer: "Na < Al < Mg < Si, because of the Be/B anomaly (here Mg/Al) of the filled subshell.",
    errors: [
      "Forgetting the anomalies: Be > B and N > O in ionization energy, and Mg > Al for the same reason.",
      "Saying the radius grows across a period: it decreases, because Zeff squeezes the electron cloud.",
      "Confusing electron affinity with electronegativity: the first is the energy of an isolated atom, the second is attraction within a bond.",
      "Forgetting that the electron affinity of N is almost zero: nitrogen has a half-filled 2p subshell and does not want another electron.",
    ],
    tip: "For any trend, ask yourself: what happens to Zeff and to shielding? If Zeff grows, the atom squeezes (radius falls, ionization rises).",
  ),
