const List<List<String>> generalTipsEn = [
  ["Method for any problem", "Data → unknown → formula → substitution → result with units. Always write in that order; if you get stuck, the missing step jumps out at you."],
  ["Convert before substituting", "Convert everything to SI units or to the units of the formula before substituting: mL → L, °C → K, g → mol. At the end, ask yourself whether the result makes sense: a pH of 25 or a molar mass of 3 g/mol for H₂SO₄ are impossible."],
  ["Stoichiometry without stumbling", "The balanced equation rules: the coefficients are the ratio in moles, never in grams. For the limiting reactant do not compare moles directly: divide the moles of each reactant by its coefficient. The smaller quotient is the limiting reactant. The yield can never exceed 100%. If you get 130%, you have swapped actual and theoretical."],
  ["Gases and solutions", "In PV = nRT the temperature is always in kelvin. It is the most frequent mistake in the whole syllabus. In dilutions, C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ only needs both volumes to be in the same unit; there is no need to convert them to liters. Molarity: the volume is that of the final solution, not of the solvent added."],
  ["Acid–base off the top of your head", "If [H⁺] = 10⁻ⁿ, the pH is directly n. If it is a×10⁻ⁿ, the pH is between n−1 and n. pH + pOH = 14 only at 25 °C. Away from that temperature, Kw changes. Diluting an acid 10 times raises the pH by 1 unit, but an acid never becomes basic by dilution: the pH approaches 7 without crossing it."],
  ["Strategy on exam day", "First pass: answer only what you can do without thinking. Mark the doubtful ones and move on; time is worth more than one question. In calculation questions, even if you do not reach the end, leave the setup written down: in university, the procedure usually earns partial credit. Save the last 5 minutes to check units, signs (ΔH, charges) and orders of magnitude. Draw an ICE table when an equilibrium or gas problem resists."],
  ["Dimensional analysis always", "Write each quantity with its unit and cancel units as if they were algebraic factors. If at the end you do not get the unit you were looking for, the setup is wrong: the unit is your free error detector."],
  ["Never round halfway through", "Keep all the decimals in the calculator and round only the final result according to the significant figures of the data (usually 3). Rounding at each step is the silent cause of most of the “almost” answers in exams."],
  ["Estimate before calculating", "Before operating, estimate the order of magnitude: should the pH be < 7? Should the volume be more or less than 22 L? If the result contradicts the estimate, look for the error before going on."],
  ["Read the question twice", "The first time to know what is going on; the second time underlining the data and the exact question. “Mass of product or moles?” and “solution or solvent?” are decided in that second reading."],
];

